79 #uuid "f06d8d45-602b-5e23-93bd-528d5726ead7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498484 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f87e3553-5a25-5197-b96d-d5ca89dea360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498493 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(4,3),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,5),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,4)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0dfaf0ac-f717-5f63-800c-8310dd062b12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498493 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(4,3),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,5),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,4),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-8-1 \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 5+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3} & ∼N(-5,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498502 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-1,7),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,9)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent and $Y={X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3},$ calculate $P(Y>22).$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ed551ba-20e8-589e-8222-2bcdb2f9bd69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498502 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-1,7),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,9),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 7+(-3)^{2}\\cdot 2+{4}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+9\\cdot 2+16\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =169.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(10,169).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(Y>22).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>22) & =P(Z>\\frac{22-10}{\\sqrt{169}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.92) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.92) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.92)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.92)≈0.8212.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>22) & =1-Φ(0.92) \\\\  & =1-0.8212 \\\\  & ≈0.1788.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498507 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(5,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,14),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,2)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent and $Y=4{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3